  "problem_statement": "freeze() method inverts behavior when called with explicit parameter\n\n#### Description\n\nThe `freeze()` method behaves incorrectly when called with an explicit boolean parameter. When `freeze(True)` is called, the dict becomes unfrozen instead of frozen, and when `freeze(False)` is called, it becomes frozen instead of unfrozen.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nfrom addict import Dict\n\n# Create a Dict and try to freeze it\nd = Dict()\nd.a = 1\n\n# This should freeze the dict but actually unfreezes it\nd.freeze(True)\nd.b = 2  # This should raise an error but doesn't\n\nprint(\"Dict after freeze(True):\", d)  # Shows both a and b\n\n# Create another Dict and try the opposite\nd2 = Dict()\nd2.x = 10\n\n# This should unfreeze the dict but actually freezes it  \nd2.freeze(False)\ntry:\n    d2.y = 20  # This should work but raises an error\nexcept AttributeError as e:\n    print(\"Error when trying to add after freeze(False):\", e)\n```\n\nThe expected behavior is that `freeze(True)` should prevent modifications and `freeze(False)` should allow them, but the actual behavior is inverted."
}